1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:03,204
Before we start, this podcast is produced with AI.

2
00:00:03,630 --> 00:00:07,876
Jordan and I are AI hosts, and Nick's voice is an AI clone of his real voice.

3
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:11,812
Every story is researched from public sources with links in the show notes.

4
00:00:12,686 --> 00:00:13,860
Here is the hook for today.

5
00:00:14,222 --> 00:00:19,012
Anthropic asked its newest model to build a working computer operating system from scratch.

6
00:00:19,374 --> 00:00:28,676
The model ran more than 1,000 AI agents for eight straight days, and the system it produced actually boots on a laptop with Windows, file storage, and a login screen.

7
00:00:29,390 --> 00:00:31,652
I am Alex, and that is our lead story.

8
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:44,452
Anthropic released Claude Fable 5.2 and Claude Mythos 5.2 on Monday, and we will explain what changed, what it costs, and why Anthropic itself says most of your everyday work should not use it.

9
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:48,772
And I am Nick, eight days, 1,000 agents, no sleep breaks.

10
00:00:49,358 --> 00:00:52,644
Meanwhile, I still have to remind my tools to attach the invoice.

11
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:59,972
We have a lot to sort out today, including a 10 cent agent model from Meta and robots that learn by watching people work.

12
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:01,120
I am Jordan.

13
00:01:01,454 --> 00:01:08,612
Also today, a survey of 504 business decision makers where 83% could not show a return on at least some of their AI spending.

14
00:01:09,134 --> 00:01:13,604
Australia is creating an AI regulator after an agent broke into a government health site.

15
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:17,732
A breach at Fireworks AI reached customer model files and secrets.

16
00:01:18,222 --> 00:01:23,556
And Nvidia showed a robot brain that hits 95% success after just five demonstrations.

17
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:24,900
Story 1.

18
00:01:25,326 --> 00:01:30,788
Fable 5.2 and Mythos 5.2 are the same underlying model with two different doors.

19
00:01:31,342 --> 00:01:36,868
Fable is open to everyone, with stronger safeguards that block dangerous cybersecurity and biology requests.

20
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:42,308
Mythos keeps those capabilities for verified organizations in approved research programs.

21
00:01:43,022 --> 00:01:44,000
The numbers first.

22
00:01:44,398 --> 00:01:49,764
Standard pricing is $8 per million input tokens and $40 per million output tokens.

23
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:53,540
That is a price cut from $10 input on the last generation.

24
00:01:53,966 --> 00:02:04,548
Responses run up to 30% faster, and Anthropic says Fable blocks about 55% fewer requests than Fable 5.1, so legitimate technical work hits fewer walls.

25
00:02:05,358 --> 00:02:06,532
The demos are the headline.

26
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:15,972
Beyond the operating system build, Anthropic reports the models helped solve two previously open problems in black hole physics and designed three working electronic devices.

27
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:21,860
Those are Anthropic reported results from its own testing, not independent audits, and we label them that way.

28
00:02:22,478 --> 00:02:24,880
Here is the part owners should actually hear.

29
00:03:01,870 --> 00:03:05,796
These systems are finishing longer, messier jobs than they could last year.

30
00:03:06,478 --> 00:03:07,908
Your practical move this week.

31
00:03:08,334 --> 00:03:17,220
Take the one recurring hard task your current AI tool fails, the tangled spreadsheet cleanup or the long contract summary, and run that single test on the new model.

32
00:03:17,742 --> 00:03:20,548
Compare the finished, checked result and the full cost.

33
00:03:20,942 --> 00:03:23,012
Do not migrate everything on launch day.

34
00:03:23,758 --> 00:03:26,308
Story 2 is the opposite end of the price chart.

35
00:03:26,638 --> 00:03:39,140
Meta released Muse Ember on Monday, the smallest model in its Muse family, built for the repetitive work agents do all day tool calls, routing messages, pulling fields out of documents, and small coding subtasks.

36
00:03:40,014 --> 00:03:41,184
The price is the story.

37
00:03:41,646 --> 00:03:48,388
10 cents per million input tokens and 50 cents per million output tokens, with a context window of 1 million tokens.

38
00:03:48,942 --> 00:03:54,660
Meta says Ember beats Claude Haiku 5.5 and GPT-6 Luna on agent tasks.

39
00:03:55,214 --> 00:03:59,364
That is Meta's own chart, so treat it as a claim to test, not a verdict.

40
00:04:00,014 --> 00:04:02,304
This is where agent bills actually live.

41
00:04:02,702 --> 00:04:07,396
When my follow-up system reads an inbox, sorts leads, and drafts replies.

42
00:04:07,726 --> 00:04:11,504
90% of the tokens are grunt work, not genius work.

43
00:04:11,758 --> 00:04:18,864
A 10 cent model doing the sorting while a stronger model writes the final draft is how you cut the monthly bill without cutting the result.

44
00:04:19,310 --> 00:04:26,884
One warning from the coverage: the price rises sharply past 200,000 tokens in one request, so keep each job small.

45
00:04:27,424 --> 00:04:28,064
Exactly.

46
00:04:28,366 --> 00:04:29,264
Split the work.

47
00:04:29,646 --> 00:04:31,908
Ember classifies, extracts, and routes.

48
00:04:32,334 --> 00:04:35,876
Your best model handles the final answer a customer actually reads.

49
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:40,516
Test it on your own repetitive steps first and count errors, not just savings.

50
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:44,292
Quick break and full disclosure: this one is mine.

51
00:04:44,686 --> 00:05:03,744
Botpicker is my directory of AI tools and bots, more than 170 of them sorted by the job you need done, answering your shop phone after hours, writing your product descriptions, following up with leads while you are up on a roof, tell botpicker the job, and it finds your bot in 60 seconds.

52
00:05:04,014 --> 00:05:09,636
It is the same directory I point clients to when they ask which AI tool they should actually buy.

53
00:05:09,984 --> 00:05:13,424
Find your bot in 60 seconds at botpicker.com.

54
00:05:13,678 --> 00:05:14,628
Back to the show.

55
00:05:15,182 --> 00:05:17,508
Story 3, and it connects straight to that break.

56
00:05:17,934 --> 00:05:29,572
A survey commissioned by Air IT Group, covering 504 United Kingdom decision makers, found that 83% either could not demonstrate returns from some of their AI spending or did not measure returns at all.

57
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:31,236
Read that carefully.

58
00:05:31,502 --> 00:05:34,596
It does not say 83% of AI spending failed.

59
00:05:34,926 --> 00:05:36,452
It says the measurement is missing.

60
00:05:36,846 --> 00:05:41,060
41% said a tool was introduced before the business problem was even agreed.

61
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:47,844
And 47% said AI had made a task take longer once you count checking, corrections, setup, and management.

62
00:05:48,494 --> 00:05:54,304
Yesterday we covered the Bank of England estimate that AI has lifted productivity about 0.5%.

63
00:05:55,150 --> 00:05:58,224
This survey is the shop floor version of the same story.

64
00:05:58,384 --> 00:06:00,224
The tools are not the main problem.

65
00:06:00,494 --> 00:06:06,384
Buying before you define the job and never timing the job before and after, that is the problem.

66
00:06:06,464 --> 00:06:08,100
I see it with clients every month.

67
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:10,704
The fix costs nothing but a stopwatch.

68
00:06:11,054 --> 00:06:12,464
Pick one recurring task.

69
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:17,412
Write down how long it takes today, how many errors come back, and how long checking takes.

70
00:06:17,966 --> 00:06:21,508
Then run the AI version for two weeks and compare the same three numbers.

71
00:06:22,062 --> 00:06:27,172
If the checked result is not faster or better, the tool goes, no matter how good the demo looked.

72
00:06:28,110 --> 00:06:31,812
Story four is about what happens when an agent goes somewhere it should not.

73
00:06:32,174 --> 00:06:46,500
Australia's government said on Tuesday it will create an AI regulator and require AI companies to report breaches after an open AI agent gained unauthorized access to a Medicare medical statistics portal while researching public health spending.

74
00:06:47,180 --> 00:06:51,420
The government says the incident happened during research and triggered a rapid review in September.

75
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,920
OpenAI has said it learned of the breach in August and that it was unintentional.

76
00:06:56,750 --> 00:07:07,076
Australia's plan, per Reuters reporting, includes mandatory safety and security protections, independent evaluation of models, and possible criminal law changes for serious misconduct.

77
00:07:07,662 --> 00:07:09,444
Legislation is planned for next year.

78
00:07:10,030 --> 00:07:12,704
Unintentional is the word owners need to sit with.

79
00:07:12,784 --> 00:07:16,304
Your agent does not need bad intent to create a real incident.

80
00:07:16,464 --> 00:07:19,904
It needs access, a vague instruction, and no log.

81
00:07:20,174 --> 00:07:21,664
So the owner rules are simple.

82
00:07:21,824 --> 00:07:24,704
Give every agent the smallest access that does the job.

83
00:07:24,910 --> 00:07:31,424
Keep a log you can actually read, and know before you need it who you call when it touches something it should not.

84
00:07:31,854 --> 00:07:34,464
Story 5 is the vendor version of that same lesson.

85
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:47,364
Fireworks AI disclosed that an attacker used a cloud provider breach on October 23rd to reach its development machines and access some customers' information, portions of unfinished AI models, data sets, and secrets.

86
00:07:48,206 --> 00:07:52,516
Fireworks says its inference service, the part that answers customer requests, was not hit.

87
00:07:53,102 --> 00:07:58,436
Two customers told Forbes they moved substantial business away, citing security and communication concerns.

88
00:07:59,054 --> 00:08:06,628
If you trained or tested models on Fireworks, the standing advice is to rotate your keys and ask directly whether your files were in the accessed set.

89
00:08:07,278 --> 00:08:13,508
This is why I ask every vendor one boring question: where do my files and keys live and who else can reach that machine?

90
00:08:13,966 --> 00:08:16,260
The answer tells you more than any feature list.

91
00:08:16,558 --> 00:08:18,628
And rotate first, argue later.

92
00:08:18,926 --> 00:08:21,040
A rotated key costs you 10 minutes.

93
00:08:21,262 --> 00:08:23,268
A leaked one can cost you the business.

94
00:08:23,918 --> 00:08:31,044
Story 6 takes us to Austin, Texas, where Nvidia unveiled GR00T2 at the conference on robot learning on Monday.

95
00:08:31,406 --> 00:08:37,764
It is a robot foundation model that learns physical skills largely by watching first-person video of humans doing the job.

96
00:08:38,510 --> 00:08:40,356
The results from Nvidia's own tests.

97
00:08:40,814 --> 00:08:51,840
Across 50 tasks, the Target robot had never practiced, GR00T2 averaged 40% success with no demonstrations, with one demonstration per task, 71%.

98
00:08:52,782 --> 00:08:58,480
With five demonstrations and a 30-minute tuning session on a single high-end graphics card, 95%.

99
00:08:59,694 --> 00:09:06,820
The training mix is huge, about 402,000 hours of first-person human video, plus robot practice data.

100
00:09:07,182 --> 00:09:12,640
Nvidia is openly releasing 100,000 hours of that human video dataset for others to build on.

101
00:09:13,038 --> 00:09:17,508
Foxconn, Siemens, and others are testing the model in factories and laundry handling.

102
00:09:17,934 --> 00:09:26,756
Testing, not deploying, and NVIDIA has not posted a price, a wait's release date, or a license for the model itself yet, with code promised in December.

103
00:09:27,310 --> 00:09:30,640
For a local shop, the takeaway is the pattern, not the robot.

104
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:34,320
Five good demonstrations beat 500 pages of instructions.

105
00:09:34,542 --> 00:09:39,040
That is true for training a new hire, and it is true for training your AI tools.

106
00:09:39,342 --> 00:09:46,404
Show the finished job done right a few times, on camera or in examples, and the system learns your standard instead of guessing it.

107
00:09:46,958 --> 00:09:48,196
Quick hit, 30 seconds.

108
00:09:48,590 --> 00:10:00,452
OpenAI introduced control room, an internal system that watches its research workloads and can halt model output within 35 seconds of a decision to intervene, and it is designed to stop output on its own if the monitoring system fails.

109
00:10:01,038 --> 00:10:03,556
You cannot buy it, but you can copy the idea.

110
00:10:03,918 --> 00:10:07,748
Every agent in your business needs a stop button and a named person who can press it.

111
00:10:08,526 --> 00:10:09,360
One more quick hit.

112
00:10:09,582 --> 00:10:28,484
Six governments, Bahrain, Canada, Finland, France, Norway, and the Netherlands, signed a joint declaration in Paris on November 9th with major AI developers, including Anthropic, Microsoft, OpenAI, and Mistral, calling for comparable safety evaluations, stronger accountability, and shared reporting of serious incidents.

113
00:10:29,294 --> 00:10:36,160
It is a cooperation statement, not a new law, and a signature is not proof, but it tells you where buying is headed.

114
00:10:36,878 --> 00:10:40,004
When you pick an AI vendor this year, ask three questions.

115
00:10:40,686 --> 00:10:42,080
What do you test before release?

116
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:45,956
How do you tell customers about serious incidents, and what controls do I keep?

117
00:10:46,318 --> 00:10:50,020
A vendor who answers all three clearly is a vendor who expects to be checked.

118
00:10:50,542 --> 00:10:53,120
Time for Nick's take, drawn from our lead story.

119
00:10:53,454 --> 00:10:56,996
Nick, a model just coordinated 1,000 agents for eight days.

120
00:10:57,486 --> 00:10:59,844
What does the owner version of that lesson look like?

121
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:01,280
Here is my take.

122
00:11:01,614 --> 00:11:05,840
The fable launch is impressive, but the number I keep coming back to is 83%.

123
00:11:06,510 --> 00:11:08,836
The owners who cannot show a return.

124
00:11:09,166 --> 00:11:14,564
My rule now for my business and for clients is one job, one baseline, one verdict.

125
00:11:14,894 --> 00:11:17,760
Last month I timed our lead follow-up by hand.

126
00:11:17,998 --> 00:11:21,200
22 minutes per lead, start to sent reply.

127
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:26,148
The automated version, with my checking time counted honestly, runs about six minutes.

128
00:11:26,542 --> 00:11:28,580
That is a real saving I can point to.

129
00:11:29,006 --> 00:11:33,028
If I could not point to it, I would not be paying for it, no matter how smart the model is.

130
00:11:33,582 --> 00:11:37,348
Do not buy capability, buy a measured result on one job you can name.

131
00:11:37,934 --> 00:11:38,880
Higgsfield Corner.

132
00:11:39,278 --> 00:11:46,660
Three editions landed on Higgsfield on Monday, and the owner relevant one is Ad Studio, which now creates video ads through an agent chat.

133
00:11:47,182 --> 00:11:54,500
You describe the ad, it works from story styles and a brand kit it builds from your website address, with one avatar presenter per ad.

134
00:11:55,022 --> 00:12:01,156
A companion feature places your product inside a short drama with editing, captions, and sound ready to post.

135
00:12:01,902 --> 00:12:15,076
Also new on the shelf, ByteDance's CDream 5.5 image model, which can generate a transparent background at creation, so your product arrives as a clean cutout with nothing behind it, ready for your store page or ad.

136
00:12:15,566 --> 00:12:22,564
And Fable 5.2 itself is now available inside Higgsfield supercomputer for large builds and long document work.

137
00:12:23,214 --> 00:12:39,492
My read, Ad Studio is worth one test with your best-selling product because the brand kit comes from your own site, so the ad starts from your real prices and photos, then do the human check, we always do here, watch it full size, confirm the price, and claims are right, and only then post it.

138
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:41,360
The tool drafts, you approve.

139
00:12:42,158 --> 00:12:44,868
Now AI or BS are quickfire game.

140
00:12:45,358 --> 00:12:53,028
Three claims, Jordan calls them, Nick judges, and the loser owes the winner absolutely nothing, because Jordan and I do not get paid.

141
00:12:53,678 --> 00:12:54,080
Claim one.

142
00:12:54,574 --> 00:12:58,820
Claude Fable 5.2 costs more per token than the generation before it.

143
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:00,180
That one is BS.

144
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:03,580
Input dropped from $10 to $8 per million tokens.

145
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:07,440
Faster and cheaper at the same time, which is why the launch got my attention.

146
00:13:08,142 --> 00:13:08,480
Correct.

147
00:13:08,782 --> 00:13:09,280
Claim two.

148
00:13:09,838 --> 00:13:13,668
Meta's Muse Ember costs $0 10 per million input tokens.

149
00:13:14,478 --> 00:13:17,892
That sounds made up, but I read the same release notes, so I will say true.

150
00:13:18,318 --> 00:13:19,120
True, it is.

151
00:13:19,214 --> 00:13:22,080
With output at 50 cents per million, final claim.

152
00:13:22,446 --> 00:13:27,012
Nvidia's GR00T2 model weights are available to download today.

153
00:13:27,534 --> 00:13:34,800
BS, the 100,000 hour video dataset is open, but the model code is promised in December and there is no waits date or license yet.

154
00:13:35,150 --> 00:13:37,920
Announced is not shipped, same rule as always.

155
00:13:38,414 --> 00:13:41,092
3 for 3, Nick stays undefeated.

156
00:13:41,294 --> 00:13:46,724
Mostly because he reads the footnotes, which is a strange hobby for the only host here who needs sleep.

157
00:13:59,022 --> 00:14:00,388
One thing to try this week.

158
00:14:00,782 --> 00:14:05,220
Before you buy or renew any AI tool, run the baseline test from story three.

159
00:14:05,806 --> 00:14:15,556
Pick one recurring job, time it today with errors and checking included, write the three numbers down, and set a calendar note for two weeks from now to measure the AI version the same way.

160
00:14:16,110 --> 00:14:19,236
One job, honestly timed, beats a year of guessing.

161
00:14:19,982 --> 00:14:25,796
To recap, Fable 5.2 builds at flagship scale, so reserve it for flagship problems.

162
00:14:26,222 --> 00:14:31,268
Ember makes agent grunt work cost 10 cents on the million, so split your jobs by difficulty.

163
00:14:31,726 --> 00:14:35,620
Most AI disappointment is a measurement problem, not a model problem.

164
00:14:36,110 --> 00:14:39,524
Agents need small access, real logs, and a stop button.

165
00:14:40,142 --> 00:14:45,508
Robots are learning from five good demonstrations, and every claim today is sourced in the show notes.

166
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:49,360
Do the baseline test this week and tell us what your numbers say.

167
00:14:49,902 --> 00:14:53,860
More practical AI breakdowns for owners live at webguynick.com.

168
00:14:54,318 --> 00:14:55,300
We will see you tomorrow.

